tion  $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$  where \n$x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117692/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117692"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection/82247e5dcd9a62b21952987c9fbc3227c85360cf70b0e2c6f29c768f54acd906" :answer/id #uuid "dbda2a72-ef8f-5988-9b68-64c69a7df8b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection/4f4377a74c14eb2c6c7d5db26b943c721352df206fea195b278e528658c350c4" :answer/id #uuid "da14f7f6-8029-5bdc-a306-d737b776c842" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection/528841bb0fe3886f594b402fc2581482b23ca9e478180a286fefed4cb6e3af9d" :answer/id #uuid "dad4c51d-e246-5e07-bbeb-a0a6d4194d3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection/2e337d02b36c8bb4d9571acd1105e4235d7800e39b1f478069a842d016dca614" :answer/id #uuid "0f7bc465-11bc-5673-9be5-989957aa1bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection/773e65e9309a1730c331d776b769da79563f4c554acb33a9ab3cc0ee256244bb" :answer/id #uuid "53d9a5e4-630b-5b85-aae8-7a3766347929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "90001df6-669f-5679-9792-d8d30e55c7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117706/selection/773e65e9309a1730c331d776b769da79563f4c554acb33a9ab3cc0ee256244bb" :answer-field/choices ["q-117706/selection/82247e5dcd9a62b21952987c9fbc3227c85360cf70b0e2c6f29c768f54acd906" "q-117706/selection/4f4377a74c14eb2c6c7d5db26b943c721352df206fea195b278e528658c350c4" "q-117706/selection/528841bb0fe3886f594b402fc2581482b23ca9e478180a286fefed4cb6e3af9d" "q-117706/selection/2e337d02b36c8bb4d9571acd1105e4235d7800e39b1f478069a842d016dca614" "q-117706/selection/773e65e9309a1730c331d776b769da79563f4c554acb33a9ab3cc0ee256244bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117706" :question/id #uuid "a3215fc2-d43b-5894-97a3-d258a0fdf783" :question/math-academy-id "q-117706" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117706/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd6703d2-36f6-5665-90e3-f75aea5f663b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
